  "slug": "stromal-bone-e93d",
  "summary": "New preprints add repeated sampling of living tissue, joint chromatin–isoform single-cell readouts and spatial causal models to cell-state mapping.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is methods for resolving cell states in tissue context, increasingly with temporal or regulatory depth. Two preprints introduce longitudinal spatial transcriptomics from living tissue: LIFE-seq samples repeatedly with nanoforest arrays to follow cortical injury in the same mouse, and LANCE-seq uses encoded microneedle arrays to profile mouse brain and track APAP-induced liver injury over time. Analytical layers extend spatial data. SpaceMACE refines tumour-cell calls using spatial proximity and expression similarity. Causal discovery on human liver Visium data models the causes and effects of senescence markers. FULL-seq jointly profiles chromatin accessibility and full-length transcripts per cell, linking promoter regulation to alternative first exon usage. A stromal strand runs alongside. Paired bulk, snRNA and chromatin profiling describes a continuous synovium–infrapatellar fat pad stromal continuum. Separately, HIPPO competency and oxidative metabolism mark human stromal clones with high bone-forming capacity. Modelling work identifies mixed feedback networks as the most robust topologies for restoring population heterogeneity.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
